1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Picture a big bowl of pasta.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:05,040
Within an hour, glucose from that meal is

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,800
pouring into your blood.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,640
Then you sleep for eight hours or more

5
00:00:08,840 --> 00:00:11,680
without eating, and your brain keeps using glucose

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:12,640
all night.

7
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,040
Yet a blood test at breakfast would usually

8
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,600
show glucose back in its normal range.

9
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,720
So what pulls glucose down after the meal,

10
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,480
and what pushes it back up while you

11
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,040
sleep?

12
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:26,746
First, three words that sound alike.

13
00:00:27,387 --> 00:00:30,266
Glucose is the sugar dissolved in your blood,

14
00:00:30,347 --> 00:00:32,506
the fuel your cells use in respiration.

15
00:00:33,227 --> 00:00:36,826
Glycogen is a store of glucose: long, branched

16
00:00:36,987 --> 00:00:38,986
chains of it packed inside liver and muscle

17
00:00:39,147 --> 00:00:39,866
cells.

18
00:00:40,187 --> 00:00:41,786
Glucagon is a hormone.

19
00:00:42,347 --> 00:00:45,466
So glycogen is the store, and glucagon is

20
00:00:45,547 --> 00:00:46,186
the messenger.

21
00:00:46,987 --> 00:00:50,146
Blood glucose is measured in millimoles per litre.

22
00:00:50,427 --> 00:00:52,026
When you haven't eaten for a while, it

23
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:54,106
usually sits around four to six.

24
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:56,826
After a meal it rises for a while,

25
00:00:56,947 --> 00:00:58,506
often to about seven or eight in a

26
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:00,986
healthy person, then comes back down.

27
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,280
Earlier in this module, you saw that the

28
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,000
pancreas is both the receptor and the control

29
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:08,440
centre for glucose.

30
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:09,640
Here's how.

31
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:13,080
Most of the pancreas makes digestive enzymes.

32
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:15,480
But scattered through it are tiny clusters of

33
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:18,520
hormone-making cells, called islets.

34
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,000
Each islet holds two cell types that matter

35
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:22,040
here.

36
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:24,760
Beta cells detect a rise in blood glucose

37
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,560
and release insulin straight into the blood.

38
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:31,160
Alpha cells detect a fall and release glucagon.

39
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:34,040
So the same small cluster senses the change

40
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:35,320
and sends the signal.

41
00:01:36,534 --> 00:01:37,573
Back to the pasta.

42
00:01:38,134 --> 00:01:41,973
As glucose rises, beta cells release more insulin,

43
00:01:42,014 --> 00:01:43,733
and it travels in the blood to cells

44
00:01:43,814 --> 00:01:45,013
all over the body.

45
00:01:45,334 --> 00:01:47,893
In muscle and fat cells, insulin binds to

46
00:01:48,014 --> 00:01:49,893
receptors on the cell surface.

47
00:01:50,134 --> 00:01:53,733
That signal moves extra glucose transporters, proteins that

48
00:01:53,814 --> 00:01:56,853
let glucose through, from inside the cell into

49
00:01:56,934 --> 00:01:57,653
its membrane.

50
00:01:58,134 --> 00:02:01,293
More doors open, so glucose moves in faster.

51
00:02:01,574 --> 00:02:05,093
In the liver, insulin switches on glycogenesis, which

52
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:08,773
means building glycogen: glucose is joined into chains

53
00:02:08,854 --> 00:02:09,733
and stored.

54
00:02:10,967 --> 00:02:14,102
Glucose leaves the blood, so blood glucose falls

55
00:02:14,167 --> 00:02:16,006
back towards its normal range.

56
00:02:16,407 --> 00:02:19,526
That's negative feedback: a rise led to a

57
00:02:19,807 --> 00:02:21,206
response that lowers it.

58
00:02:21,447 --> 00:02:24,246
And as glucose falls, the beta cells detect

59
00:02:24,327 --> 00:02:26,646
less of a rise, so they release less

60
00:02:26,727 --> 00:02:27,566
insulin.

61
00:02:27,847 --> 00:02:30,646
Insulin drops back to a low background level,

62
00:02:30,727 --> 00:02:31,526
not to zero.

63
00:02:32,167 --> 00:02:35,286
Notice that insulin doesn't destroy glucose.

64
00:02:35,327 --> 00:02:38,166
The glucose is used in respiration or stored.

65
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,686
Now the night.

66
00:02:40,967 --> 00:02:43,926
Hours after your last meal, cells keep using

67
00:02:44,007 --> 00:02:47,446
glucose, and blood glucose starts to drift down.

68
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:50,246
Alpha cells detect the fall and release more

69
00:02:50,327 --> 00:02:51,246
glucagon.

70
00:02:51,527 --> 00:02:53,606
Glucagon travels mainly to the liver.

71
00:02:54,167 --> 00:02:57,846
There it switches on glycogenolysis, which means splitting

72
00:02:57,927 --> 00:03:01,046
glycogen: the stored chains are broken back into

73
00:03:01,127 --> 00:03:03,726
glucose, and liver cells release it into the

74
00:03:03,767 --> 00:03:04,166
blood.

75
00:03:04,647 --> 00:03:07,526
Blood glucose rises back into range.

76
00:03:08,007 --> 00:03:11,046
Muscle stores glycogen too, but muscle keeps its

77
00:03:11,127 --> 00:03:13,766
glucose for itself, so on this side the

78
00:03:13,847 --> 00:03:15,126
liver does the main work.

79
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:18,053
Why two hormones?

80
00:03:18,334 --> 00:03:20,413
Think of driving at a steady speed over

81
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:21,133
hills.

82
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:24,013
Going downhill you speed up, so you press

83
00:03:24,054 --> 00:03:24,573
the brake.

84
00:03:25,054 --> 00:03:27,533
Going uphill you slow down, so you press

85
00:03:27,614 --> 00:03:28,493
the accelerator.

86
00:03:29,134 --> 00:03:32,253
Insulin works like the brake on rising glucose,

87
00:03:32,334 --> 00:03:35,413
and glucagon like the accelerator when it falls.

88
00:03:35,694 --> 00:03:37,773
With only one pedal, you could ease off

89
00:03:37,854 --> 00:03:40,093
it, but you couldn't actively push the other

90
00:03:40,174 --> 00:03:40,973
way.

91
00:03:41,454 --> 00:03:44,653
Where the analogy breaks: both hormones are released

92
00:03:44,734 --> 00:03:46,973
at some level all the time, and it's

93
00:03:47,054 --> 00:03:49,133
the balance between them that shifts.

94
00:03:50,334 --> 00:03:52,293
Two things examiners look for.

95
00:03:52,574 --> 00:03:56,093
First, the pancreas detects and signals, but it

96
00:03:56,174 --> 00:03:58,413
doesn't change blood glucose itself.

97
00:03:59,054 --> 00:04:02,253
The effectors are the cells that move glucose:

98
00:04:02,294 --> 00:04:05,133
liver, muscle and fat cells, with the liver

99
00:04:05,214 --> 00:04:07,533
doing most of the work when glucose falls.

100
00:04:08,174 --> 00:04:11,173
Second, on a graph, the hormone follows the

101
00:04:11,214 --> 00:04:11,933
glucose.

102
00:04:12,414 --> 00:04:15,933
Glucose rises first, and insulin rises in response.

103
00:04:16,574 --> 00:04:19,613
If you write that insulin made glucose rise,

104
00:04:19,694 --> 00:04:21,613
you've swapped cause and response.

105
00:04:22,834 --> 00:04:26,113
To keep the pair straight: insulin lets glucose

106
00:04:26,194 --> 00:04:26,913
in.

107
00:04:26,994 --> 00:04:29,713
Glucagon acts when glucose has gone.

108
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:32,433
And B for beta cells, which bring blood

109
00:04:32,514 --> 00:04:33,633
glucose down.

110
00:04:34,834 --> 00:04:36,513
Two exam questions.

111
00:04:36,834 --> 00:04:37,513
First.

112
00:04:37,794 --> 00:04:41,073
A student eats breakfast at seven, skips lunch,

113
00:04:41,154 --> 00:04:43,153
and runs a cross-country race at two in

114
00:04:43,194 --> 00:04:44,513
the afternoon.

115
00:04:44,594 --> 00:04:47,073
Their blood glucose begins to fall.

116
00:04:47,314 --> 00:04:49,873
Construct a negative feedback loop that returns blood

117
00:04:49,954 --> 00:04:52,433
glucose to normal, and explain the role of

118
00:04:52,474 --> 00:04:53,553
the liver.

119
00:04:53,714 --> 00:04:55,073
Pause here to answer.

120
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,420
Stimulus: blood glucose falls.

121
00:05:08,700 --> 00:05:11,740
Receptor and control centre: alpha cells in the

122
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:13,740
pancreas, which release glucagon.

123
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,420
Effector: the liver, which breaks glycogen down to

124
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:19,740
glucose and releases it into the blood.

125
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:24,380
Response: blood glucose rises back towards normal, opposing

126
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:24,940
the fall.

127
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:28,220
Writing that glucagon breaks down glucose is the

128
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:29,500
classic mistake.

129
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:33,260
The liver breaks down glycogen and releases glucose.

130
00:05:34,467 --> 00:05:34,946
Second.

131
00:05:35,427 --> 00:05:38,546
After an overnight fast, a healthy adult drank

132
00:05:38,587 --> 00:05:40,066
a glucose solution.

133
00:05:40,387 --> 00:05:42,786
The graph shows their blood glucose and blood

134
00:05:42,947 --> 00:05:45,026
insulin over three hours.

135
00:05:45,427 --> 00:05:47,746
Explain the change in insulin over the first

136
00:05:47,827 --> 00:05:51,106
hour, and explain why blood glucose returned to

137
00:05:51,267 --> 00:05:54,066
about its starting level, with reference to negative

138
00:05:54,147 --> 00:05:55,026
feedback.

139
00:05:55,347 --> 00:05:56,706
Pause here to answer.

140
00:06:07,134 --> 00:06:09,693
Glucose rose as the drink was absorbed.

141
00:06:10,174 --> 00:06:13,213
Beta cells detected the rise, so insulin rose

142
00:06:13,294 --> 00:06:14,493
within thirty minutes.

143
00:06:15,134 --> 00:06:17,533
Insulin made muscle and fat cells take up

144
00:06:17,614 --> 00:06:20,413
glucose and the liver store it as glycogen,

145
00:06:20,494 --> 00:06:21,693
so glucose fell.

146
00:06:22,254 --> 00:06:25,373
As it fell, beta cells released less insulin,

147
00:06:25,494 --> 00:06:27,213
so both lines settled.

148
00:06:27,694 --> 00:06:30,013
Saying the pancreas took up the glucose is

149
00:06:30,094 --> 00:06:33,373
the classic mistake: the pancreas only sends the

150
00:06:33,774 --> 00:06:34,013
signal.

151
00:06:35,234 --> 00:06:38,593
So for any glucose question, ask which way

152
00:06:38,674 --> 00:06:42,433
glucose moved, which cells detected it, which hormone

153
00:06:42,514 --> 00:06:44,673
they released, and what the liver or other

154
00:06:44,754 --> 00:06:45,873
cells then did.

155
00:06:46,594 --> 00:06:48,513
Next, the same kind of loop with a

156
00:06:48,594 --> 00:06:51,793
different variable: how your kidneys control the water

157
00:06:51,874 --> 00:06:52,673
in your blood.

158
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:55,340
Here are the key notes.

159
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:57,500
Pause the video if you need more time

160
00:06:57,580 --> 00:06:58,380
to copy them.
